{"kind":"report","text":"APPROVED at exact head e19b9ab21e8a95a7665cf64742d7d762cc3b4d8e (direct child of main 23c4c759, pushed as origin/task/linked-worktrees; branch prefix task/ is the one the assigning request names). Diff versus merge base is test-only: +118 lines across cmd/gs/main_test.go and cmd/gitseq-mcp/main_test.go, purely additive, no production code and no documentation change. This corroborates the ready report: current main already resolves repository-scoped config, custody and refs through the Git common directory, and this head supplies the missing command-level regressions.\n\nARCHITECTURE. Affected layers are cmd/gs (Surface and composition) and cmd/gitseq-mcp (Surface) in docs/reference/architecture.md. The head adds no production code, so it changes no layer contract and architecture.md needs no update at this head. The behaviour the tests pin is owned by internal/app (Application host and boundary adapter): ResolveGitDirs separates the selected checkout's GitDir from the repository's CommonDir, and MetaDir, the object store and actor custody hang off CommonDir. The tests bind that separation from the two surfaces using exported app entry points only, without reaching into kernel or workroom internals, so the surface-to-host boundary is preserved rather than widened. The head touches neither internal/app nor internal/kernel.\n\nSECURITY. No trust or authority boundary moves: a test-only head introduces no untrusted input path, signature, secret, bound or failure mode. I examined the specific risk that this coverage could mask an unsafe worktree resolution, and it does not. ResolveGitDirs delegates wholly to git rev-parse --path-format=absolute --absolute-git-dir --git-common-dir, so following a .git file is git's own resolution under git's trust model, not a bespoke parser that new tests could bless. The tests assert CommonDir agreement only across two checkouts of one repository they create themselves; nothing asserts that a .git file redirecting outside the repository is honoured, so no unsafe redirect is entrenched. Hermeticity verified from source: the adapter discovers a resident by reading CommonDir/gitseq/resident.json and refuses it on genesis mismatch (internal/app/resident.go), never dialling a fixed port, so the live resident on 127.0.0.1:7777 cannot influence the run; the resident-unavailable notice in the test output is itself evidence the adapter resolved the temp repository's common directory. No test binds a port, none calls t.Parallel, none touches this repository's own worktrees, and all filesystem effects stay inside t.TempDir(). Workroom commits pin identity and signing configuration explicitly, so nothing depends on global git identity or the user's allowed-signers file.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION. Nothing to cut. Both tests follow existing local fixtures (initRepository in cmd/gitseq-mcp, newWorkflowFixtureRemoving in cmd/gs) rather than inventing a new shape, and the two are not redundant with one another: the gs test covers status, verify and state, while the MCP test additionally exercises attach and the actor check through the common directory. Every assertion is load-bearing, proved by mutation rather than assumed.\n\nMUTATION EVIDENCE (throwaway worktree, reverted; worktree confirmed clean at the exact head afterwards). Mutating the seam internal/app.ResolveGitDirs in both directions turns both new tests red: returning (paths[0], paths[0]), collapsing the common directory onto the git directory, fails with the linked checkout seeking config at .git/worktrees/linked/gitseq/config.json; returning (paths[1], paths[1]), collapsing the git directory onto the common directory, fails on the scope-separation assertion. Both halves of the claimed property are genuinely bound, and neither assertion is decoration.\n\nFINDING, recorded but not blocking. One adjacent invariant is left uncovered. cmd/gitseq-mcp/main.go:624-631 states that a linked worktree is a checkout of a repository rather than a second workroom, and that two paths sharing a common git directory share one attachment and one cached projection; attach implements this by keying rooms on byCommonDir. The new MCP test constructs two separate mcpServer instances, so it cannot reach that path, and the pre-existing tests populate byCommonDir by hand instead of driving attach. I removed the sharing, making attach allocate a fresh room every time so one adapter process would hold two divergent cached projections of one repository, and the entire cmd/gitseq-mcp suite still passed. I approve nonetheless: the conditions of satisfaction are met as written, proving gs status/verify/state and gitseq-mcp startup from main and from linked worktrees; the head is test-only and strictly improves on main; and the gap is pre-existing rather than introduced here. It merits a child request, since roughly five lines driving one server's attach across both checkouts and asserting the two returned rooms are identical would close it.\n\nMERGE NOTE for whoever merges. Three live artifacts cover the changed paths and must be retired at step 5: cmd/gs at 23c4c759 (event ending 29d1ac3f44f6) and, at cmd/gitseq-mcp, both 48edf0cbdd19 and the stale-but-still-live 29212a076024, since stale is not retired and a stale artifact still occupies its path.\n\nGATES at the exact head, go build ./... \u0026\u0026 go vet ./... \u0026\u0026 go test ./... -count=1, verbatim:\nBUILD OK\nVET OK\nok  github.com/generalbusiness-ai/gitseq 0.206s\nok  github.com/generalbusiness-ai/gitseq/cmd/gitseq-github 0.300s\nok  github.com/generalbusiness-ai/gitseq/cmd/gitseq-mcp 146.997s\nok  github.com/generalbusiness-ai/gitseq/cmd/gitseq-perf 0.304s\nok  github.com/generalbusiness-ai/gitseq/cmd/gs 274.020s\nok  github.com/generalbusiness-ai/gitseq/internal/app 140.644s\nok  github.com/generalbusiness-ai/gitseq/internal/connector/github 1.666s\nok  github.com/generalbusiness-ai/gitseq/internal/custody 3.638s\nok  github.com/generalbusiness-ai/gitseq/inte